<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Formulating proportional and capacity constraints for an organic mixture</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A group of women farmers in Makueni managing a climate-resilient greenhouse project needs to prepare a liquid organic foliar feed mixture. They combine concentrated neem extract liquid (\(x\) liters) with an aloe vera solution base (\(y\) liters).</p>
<ul>
<li>The mixing tank has a maximum storage capacity of <b>{@C@} liters</b>.</li>
<li>To guarantee effective pest control efficacy, the volume of neem extract liquid (\(x\)) must be <b>at least {@R@} times</b> the volume of the aloe vera solution base (\(y\)).</li>
</ul>

<p><b>a) </b> Write the linear inequalities that model the physical capacity and the chemical proportion limitations. <i>(Use \( \le \) or \( \ge \) as appropriate. Do not include the non-negativity constraints \(x \ge 0, y \ge 0\)).</i></p>

<p>Tank capacity constraint: [[input:ans1]]</p>
<p>Mixture proportion constraint: [[input:ans2]]</p>

<div><b>Capacity check:</b> [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<div><b>Proportion check:</b> [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> The farmers decide to pour exactly <b>{@y_val@} liters</b> of aloe vera solution (\(y\)) into the mixing tank.</p>
<p>Based on your established inequalities, determine the allowable range for the neem extract (\(x\)):</p>

<p>1. What is the absolute <b>minimum</b> volume of neem extract required to satisfy the pest control efficacy proportion?</p>
<p>Minimum neem: [[input:ans3]] liters</p>
<div><b>Minimum check:</b> [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>

<p>2. What is the absolute <b>maximum</b> volume of neem extract they can add without overflowing the mixing tank?</p>
<p>Maximum neem: [[input:ans4]] liters</p>
<div><b>Maximum check:</b> [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part a: Formulating the Constraints</b></p>
<p>1. <b>Tank Capacity Constraint:</b> The sum of both liquids cannot exceed the maximum volumetric size of the container. "Maximum capacity" implies less than or equal to (\(\le\)):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@ta1@}} \)</p>

<p>2. <b>Mixture Proportion Constraint:</b> The volume of neem (\(x\)) must be greater than or equal to \({@R@}\) times the aloe vera volume (\(y\)):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@ta2@}} \)</p>
<p><i>(Note: This can also be correctly rearranged and written as \( x - {@R@}y \ge 0 \)).</i></p>

<p><b>Part b: Evaluating Operational Limits</b></p>
<p>We are given that \( y = {@y_val@} \). We must substitute this value into both inequalities to find the lower and upper bounds for \(x\).</p>

<p><b>1. Finding the Minimum (Efficacy Constraint):</b><br>
Substitute \( y = {@y_val@} \) into \( x \ge {@R@}y \):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \begin{aligned}
x &amp;\ge {@R@}({@y_val@}) \\
x &amp;\ge {@ta3@}
\end{aligned} \)
</p>
<p>The absolute minimum amount of neem extract required is <b>{@ta3@} liters</b>.</p>

<p><b>2. Finding the Maximum (Capacity Constraint):</b><br>
Substitute \( y = {@y_val@} \) into \( x + y \le {@C@} \):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \begin{aligned}
x + {@y_val@} &amp;\le {@C@} \\
x &amp;\le {@C@} - {@y_val@} \\
x &amp;\le {@ta4@}
\end{aligned} \)
</p>
<p>The absolute maximum amount of neem extract that will safely fit is <b>{@ta4@} liters</b>.</p>

<p>Therefore, when using {@y_val@} liters of aloe vera, the farmers must add between {@ta3@} and {@ta4@} liters of neem extract to run a successful operation.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
